What if I don't know how many rows there are? If your data is paginated, you can render one more row than you have loaded for as long as there are more pages to load. When that row is scrolled into view, it will trigger loading the next page. function Example({
 hasNextPage,
 rows
}: {
 hasNextPage: boolean;
 rows: string[];
}) {
 // Render an extra row while more rows are available;
 // scrolling it into view will trigger loading the next page
 const rowCount = hasNextPage ? rows.length + 1 : rows.length;

 const onRowsRendered = useInfiniteLoader({
 isRowLoaded,
 loadMoreRows,
 rowCount
 });

 return (
 <List
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ rows }}
 />
 );
} Alternately, rowCount can be set to an arbitrarily high number.

What happens if loading rows fails? Rows are considered pending until the Promise returned by loadMoreRows settles. After that, rows that are still not loaded will be requested again the next time they are rendered. Errors should be handled within loadMoreRows, for example: async function loadMoreRows(startIndex: number, stopIndex: number) {
 try {
 const rows = await fetchRows(startIndex, stopIndex);
 storeRows(startIndex, rows);
 } catch (error) {
 // These rows will be requested again the next time they are rendered
 showError(error);
 }
}

